> I recently spent a few weeks debating with the leftists in
> alt.society.anarchy.  The principal complaint of at least some of
> them is that propertarians monopolize land by force.  A capitalist
> enclave would necessarily prevent some people from exercise their
> natural right to walk wherever they please.

  Is the prevention of transportation through some area sufficient to destroy 
an anarchosyndicate? I mean, must an anarchosyndicalist be able to
stand in any point in the universe before he is truly free, or before
his form of society can work? That doesn't seem to be a very robust
system.

  In fact, I remember debating with some leftists and I asked them why
the USSR and China failed. The response was, capitalists still owned
a lot of land. It seems that communism/socialism are sensitive enough
to external pressure that even the mere existence of a capitalist
enclave somewhere would result in the failure of them. Now, the
opposite is not true. An anarchosyndicate sitting next to, or contained
within an anarchocapital would not harm the AC. So from
a prisoner's dilemma perspective, defection cannot be allowed
globally because it could harm unstable left-anarchies. In the long
run then, a left anarchy is inherently unstable because 1) being an anarchy,
it can not prevent black markets (in goods and services) (else, it
wouldn't be an anarchy) and 2) the existence of such markets leads
to the downfall of the syndicate (or atleast its "purity" which in
the minds of many AS'ers means a failure). A resource argument is irrelevent.
Hong Kong doesn't have many resources yet it still prospers. The USSR
had tremendous natural resources and still failed.

  If the left-anarchists believe that their society has to be based on an
all-or-nothing approach (that is, either no land is owned, or their
society won't work), then they will never achieve a working form of
their society. Unless of course, they institute a world anti-government
somehow, and then continue to prevent local peoples from establishing
some form of land ownership -- which would mean that they have in
fact, established a state to maintain an unstable order, canceling
out the 'anarchy'

-Ray

