Noot yet, but I'm working on it.  I've been on a mailing list
discussing intentional communities for quite a while, and have done
some research into communal/tribal living, adding in my experience
from heavy involvement in dining co-ops in college (unlike the
Berkeley almost-co-ops where long-term non-student paid administrators
take care of most of the paperwork).  I've come to several conclusions,
and want to do some actual travel and visiting once my current work
project is over.

1) Do it to have fun.  Cause-based communities burn themselves out fast.
2) Select membership carefully based on shared goals.  Don't be afraid
to be elitist; close living involves intimacy comparable to a group
marriage.  The more you agree on basics, the less time spent arguing
about them.
3) We're looking for an improvement on life in traditional
society, so let's push some limits, ok?
4) What's the plan for self-sufficiency so people don't have to take
rotating urban shitwork shifts to pay bills?
5) What else is important here?  "How can we live together and not
hate or kill each other too much?" is possibly the hardest question
we face -- humans have shown themselves to do just fine with technology,
but the next step in social evolution is not clear, and the current
nuclear (or less) family is better suited to maintaining artificially 
high demand for consumer goods than having a fulfilling life.

I haven't thought about this in a while, so there's still some rust
on the gears.

